# Expectation record for 05_pointers/010_qualified_pointers.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 010_qualified_pointers
area               = 05_pointers
description        = const and volatile qualified pointers and qualified pointed-to types, with volatile reads forcing genuine loads
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
const is NEVER cast away and then written through. Every write goes to an object that is
not itself const: plain, mpair and the volatile objects vval, varr, vcounter and vsink are
all declared without const. The genuinely const objects cval, carr, cvval and cpair are
only ever read, through const-qualified pointers. Adding a qualifier to a pointer type is
an implicit, legal conversion and is used in that direction only, never the reverse. Every
index is inside bounds: carr and varr have 5 elements indexed 0..4. The volatile counter
arithmetic stays far inside int range. Writing through a const-qualified POINTER to a
non-const object, as in int *const, is legal and is what the bump helper does.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
volatile only constrains how accesses are performed, never what values result, so a
compiler that emits more or fewer loads still produces identical output. Every volatile
read and write in this program is to an object whose value is fully determined by earlier
statements in the same program, so the printed values are deterministic.
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d and %lld, both of which are
target-width independent.
END
